  "response_text": "Setting up a thread\u2019s stack means preparing the memory space the thread will use to store its state, including the values of key registers. For a thread to start correctly, we push values like PC, LR, and general-purpose registers onto its stack in the format expected by the CPU during context switching. This allows the CPU to \"restore\" the thread's state when it starts running. Register values must be stored on the stack because they represent the execution context\u2014without them, the CPU wouldn\u2019t know where to resume or how to continue the thread\u2019s execution safely and correctly.\\n",
